\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Transpose as an ordinary involution}
\date{2026-10-31}

\p{Let #{R} be a commutative unital ring and #{A=M_n(R)}. Define
#{(X^T)_{ij}=X_{ji}}. This gives an
[ordinary linear involution](fdip-001G):
##{(rX+Y)^T=rX^T+Y^T,\qquad I^T=I,\qquad
(XY)^T=Y^TX^T,\qquad (X^T)^T=X.}}
\proof{
\p{The first, second and fourth identities follow by evaluating the
#{(i,j)} entry. For product reversal, commutativity gives
##{\begin{aligned}
((XY)^T)_{ij}&=\sum_k X_{jk}Y_{ki}\\
&=\sum_k Y_{ki}X_{jk}=(Y^TX^T)_{ij}.
\end{aligned}}
The same formulas include #{n=0}, with empty sums.}
}
\p{For instance, transposing #{\left(\begin{smallmatrix}a&b\\c&d\end{smallmatrix}\right)}
exchanges #{b} and #{c}. Over #{\mathbb C}, transpose is complex-linear,
whereas conjugate transpose is not: #{(iI)^*=-iI\ne iI^*}
when #{n>0}. Conjugate transpose is nevertheless real-linear, so the
coefficient ring matters. The [counting adjoint](fdip-001H) recovers
ordinary transpose from its specified bilinear pairing.}
\p{These are entrywise deductions from the definition. Mathlib's
\leanref{Matrix.transpose_add}, \leanref{Matrix.transpose_smul},
\leanref{Matrix.transpose_mul} and \leanref{Matrix.transpose_transpose}
record the corresponding identities; see \citek{mathlib2020lean}.}
